WEBVTT

1
00:00:07.881 --> 00:00:14.727
Hello, everyone!

2
00:00:15.087 --> 00:00:22.533
Welcome to another episode of Superstars in History, the talk show featuring exclusive interviews with people from the

3
00:00:22.613 --> 00:00:25.416
past. I'm your host, Faye Moss.

4
00:00:26.256 --> 00:00:36.044
Today's guest was both a compassionate nurse and a gifted statistician who made groundbreaking contributions to healthcare.

5
00:00:36.064 --> 00:00:41.466
Please welcome the one and only Lady with the Lamp—Florence Nightingale!

6
00:00:57.850 --> 00:00:58.891
Thank you, Faye!

7
00:00:59.571 --> 00:01:03.092
We can't wait to hear all about your career, Florence.

8
00:01:04.024 --> 00:01:06.565
Did you always dream about being a nurse?

9
00:01:07.845 --> 00:01:13.407
Well, as a child, I did enjoy taking care of family pets when they were injured.

10
00:01:14.227 --> 00:01:20.768
I was also intrigued by my relatives' ailments and kept a written record of things like my sister's

11
00:01:20.868 --> 00:01:23.329
toothache and an uncle's back pain!

12
00:01:24.269 --> 00:01:31.391
However, when I was born in 1820, females in Great Britain didn't think much about careers.

13
00:01:32.292 --> 00:01:37.947
Unless a woman was poor, she was just expected to get married and raise a family.

14
00:01:38.748 --> 00:01:39.668
Did you go to school?

15
00:01:40.929 --> 00:01:46.854
No, but my family was quite wealthy, so my sister and I had a governess who gave us

16
00:01:46.954 --> 00:01:54.580
lessons. And my father tutored us in challenging subjects like physics, Greek, and statistics.

17
00:01:55.400 --> 00:02:03.550
In our household we were constantly discussing important issues, so I grew up yearning to do something meaningful

18
00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:04.391
with my life.

19
00:02:05.131 --> 00:02:10.095
At age 16, I had a religious awakening that affirmed this desire.

20
00:02:11.035 --> 00:02:11.696
What do you mean?

21
00:02:12.696 --> 00:02:18.060
I had a strong feeling that God was calling on me to reduce human suffering.

22
00:02:18.941 --> 00:02:22.363
I eventually came to believe this meant becoming a nurse.

23
00:02:23.544 --> 00:02:25.765
I bet your parents were proud of you!

24
00:02:26.686 --> 00:02:29.808
Quite the opposite, Faye—they were horrified!

25
00:02:30.544 --> 00:02:35.546
At the time most hospitals were filthy places where the poor went for treatment.

26
00:02:36.547 --> 00:02:43.229
Nurses were like servants who performed menial tasks such as emptying chamber pots and mopping floors.

27
00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:48.992
My parents hoped I'd forget my foolish ideas and marry one of my suitors!

28
00:02:49.652 --> 00:02:53.233
But I clung stubbornly to my dream for seven years.

29
00:02:54.074 --> 00:02:59.576
In 1851 I finally enrolled in an excellent nursing school in Germany.

30
00:03:00.664 --> 00:03:05.648
After my training I took a job running a hospital for women in London.

31
00:03:06.449 --> 00:03:11.133
And then in 1854, Britain went to war in Europe.

32
00:03:12.534 --> 00:03:14.695
Oh, was that the Crimean War?

33
00:03:15.876 --> 00:03:21.521
Yes. British troops went to Crimea to stop Russian expansion in that area.

34
00:03:22.241 --> 00:03:28.506
The ill or wounded soldiers were sent to a hospital across the Black Sea in Scutari, Turkey.

35
00:03:29.287 --> 00:03:35.355
Later that year I was put in charge of a team of 38 other nurses and dispatched to

36
00:03:35.435 --> 00:03:39.718
Scutari. What we saw when we arrived was shocking.

37
00:03:40.518 --> 00:03:47.963
Along with the soldiers' battle wounds, illnesses such as frostbite, dysentery, and cholera were rampant.

38
00:03:48.803 --> 00:03:56.728
Conditions inside the hospital were ghastly, with overcrowding, broken plumbing, and rats scurrying around.

39
00:04:00.076 --> 00:04:02.238
Did you say rats?

40
00:04:03.659 --> 00:04:08.943
Yes, rats, not to mention infestations of fleas and lice!

41
00:04:09.783 --> 00:04:15.588
Soldiers were sleeping on the floor without blankets, and food and medical supplies were scarce.

42
00:04:16.728 --> 00:04:21.552
During my first winter at Scutari, over four thousand men died.

43
00:04:22.793 --> 00:04:24.074
How terrible.

44
00:04:24.734 --> 00:04:32.527
It was indeed terrible, and in 1855, the British government sent a sanitary commission to investigate.

45
00:04:33.347 --> 00:04:39.090
The commission confirmed my suspicions that the filthy conditions were affecting the soldiers' health.

46
00:04:39.951 --> 00:04:49.416
Once the hospital began to focus on sanitation—cleaning sewers, improving ventilation, and scrubbing everything—patients' health improved.

47
00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:57.085
In just a few months, the mortality rate dropped from 42.7 percent to 2.2 percent.

48
00:04:58.105 --> 00:04:59.847
That's impressive, Florence!

49
00:05:00.907 --> 00:05:02.628
It certainly wasn't easy.

50
00:05:03.149 --> 00:05:10.214
The work never ended, and many men disliked working with women—especially one as stubborn as me!

51
00:05:11.494 --> 00:05:14.977
I also traveled to the front lines to care for sick soldiers.

52
00:05:15.617 --> 00:05:22.799
During one such trip, I contracted Crimean Fever, a disease that causes pain and exhaustion.

53
00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:30.446
I was quite ill but kept working; I refused to go home until the last British patients were

54
00:05:30.506 --> 00:05:31.167
sent home.

55
00:05:32.088 --> 00:05:35.131
When did you get your nickname—the Lady with the Lamp?

56
00:05:35.591 --> 00:05:37.453
Was it during the Crimean War?

57
00:05:38.374 --> 00:05:43.779
Yes, the soldiers called me that because I carried a lamp when I checked on them at night,

58
00:05:43.959 --> 00:05:44.760
in the darkness.

59
00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:52.004
The nickname gained popularity in England after a newspaper featured an illustration of me with my lamp.

60
00:05:52.564 --> 00:05:59.709
By the time I arrived home in 1856, I was quite famous—Queen Victoria even sent me a thank

61
00:05:59.729 --> 00:06:00.169
you note!

62
00:06:00.689 --> 00:06:03.691
However, fame didn't interest me one bit.

63
00:06:04.291 --> 00:06:07.213
I just wanted to be left alone to continue working.

64
00:06:08.314 --> 00:06:09.154
How was your health?

65
00:06:09.654 --> 00:06:12.076
Were you able to continue with your nursing career?

66
00:06:12.988 --> 00:06:18.893
No, unfortunately, the Crimean Fever had left me in almost constant pain.

67
00:06:20.995 --> 00:06:25.999
But I found a way to use my knowledge of statistics to improve medical care.

68
00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:34.126
I'd taken extensive notes during the war, and a colleague and I began analyzing data about military hospitals

69
00:06:34.326 --> 00:06:36.829
for a report on the health of the British army.

70
00:06:37.849 --> 00:06:43.097
The report, released in 1858, was over five hundred pages long.

71
00:06:43.777 --> 00:06:49.920
It caused quite a stir because I was one of the first researchers to use diagrams in addition

72
00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:53.822
to statistics to illustrate important trends in medical care.

73
00:06:54.702 --> 00:06:56.903
Did it lead to changes in healthcare?

74
00:06:57.644 --> 00:06:59.044
It most certainly did.

75
00:06:59.565 --> 00:07:08.521
From my data, lawmakers could now clearly see, for example, that sanitation was hugely important; more British soldiers

76
00:07:08.601 --> 00:07:11.644
died from preventable causes than from battle wounds!

77
00:07:12.925 --> 00:07:17.869
My work led to cleaner hospitals and even a new sewer system in London.

78
00:07:18.790 --> 00:07:24.535
I also became the first female fellow in the Royal Statistical Society—quite an honor.

79
00:07:25.396 --> 00:07:26.016
I'll say!

80
00:07:26.377 --> 00:07:28.638
Didn't you establish a nursing school too?

81
00:07:29.699 --> 00:07:33.903
Yes, the Nightingale School of Nursing opened in London in 1860.

82
00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:43.485
We trained nurses and instituted practices that became standard, such as frequent hand-washing and wearing uniforms.

83
00:07:44.485 --> 00:07:51.629
Each year I sent a letter to the students, gently reminding them to maintain important nursing principles such

84
00:07:51.689 --> 00:07:55.111
as kindness, sympathy, and trustworthiness.

85
00:07:56.031 --> 00:08:03.576
I also continued researching aspects of medical care, including ways to improve working and living conditions for the

86
00:08:03.636 --> 00:08:06.750
poor and recommendations for hospital design.

87
00:08:07.611 --> 00:08:14.116
Wow! At a time when most women didn't have careers, you revolutionized the field of nursing!

88
00:08:14.837 --> 00:08:19.141
And you fulfilled your personal ambition to reduce suffering in the world.

89
00:08:19.661 --> 00:08:21.583
We'll let you return to history now.

90
00:08:22.504 --> 00:08:35.545
Good-bye! Nightingale was a talented nurse and a remarkable statistician whose research changed healthcare forever.

91
00:08:37.026 --> 00:08:38.987
She died in 1910.

92
00:08:40.107 --> 00:08:44.309
Her accomplishments also inspired many other women to become nurses.

93
00:08:45.369 --> 00:08:51.692
International Nurses Day is celebrated each year on Florence Nightingale's birthday, May 12.

94
00:08:52.532 --> 00:08:55.333
I hope you enjoyed my interview with Florence Nightingale.

95
00:08:56.014 --> 00:08:59.775
I'll be back soon with another fascinating guest from the past.

96
00:09:00.335 --> 00:09:04.930
You've been watching Faye Moss on Superstars in History!
